{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>This they shall give, everyone who passes over the counted — half a shekel, etc.</b> Rashi explained: the way of those who count is to pass the counted ones one after another, etc. Yet in any case the expression ״עַל הַפְּקוּדִים״ ('over the counted') does not bear this meaning. And it is possible to explain according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Jerusalemite Talmud, Shekalim chapter 2, halakhah 3) that this was an atonement for the sin of the calf, for there Israel transgressed the upright commandments of YHWH that HaShem had commanded Moses. Therefore it says ״כָּל הָעוֹבֵר עַל הַפְּקוּדִים״ — they are transgressors of transgression who needed this atonement." "And the reason for 'half a shekel' — several different explanations are found in the midrashim, and I say to add to them three more reasons. The first is this: because in the matter of the calf they caused the breaking of the tablets into two, therefore they shall give a beka', that is, a shekel split into two. The second is this: because this was an atonement for the soul, and it is known that from the side of the soul no person has any advantage over another, for the vital soul is equal in all; therefore rich and poor are equal in this atonement. And since every person is a whole shekel, and every half is included in the number ten — for thus our Rabbis of blessed memory counted (Niddah 31a) in the statement 'three partners are in man': ten things come from the father and the mother, and ten things come from the Holy One, blessed be He — if so, the whole man is twenty gerah, and half of it is a terumah to YHWH, to atone for the portion of the soul." "The third reason is the most lucid of all, and it is this: we find in the midrash (Tanhuma, Ki Tisa 11), and the Yalkut brings it in this portion, and this is its language: ״וְנָתְנוּ אִישׁ כֹּפֶר נַפְשׁוֹ״. When Moses heard this he was afraid and said, ״עוֹר בְּעַד עוֹר וְכֹל אֲשֶׁר לָאִישׁ יִתֵּן בְּעַד נַפְשׁוֹ״ (Job 2:4). Rabbi Yehudah bar Ilai says: Moses said, we find that the redemption of a man's soul is a talent of silver, as it says (I Kings 20:39), ״וְהָיְתָה נַפְשְׁךָ תַּחַת נַפְשׁוֹ אוֹ כִּכַּר כֶּסֶף תִּשְׁקוֹל״. Rabbi Yosei says: you learn from one who spreads an evil report, as it says (Deuteronomy 22:19), ״וְעָנְשׁוּ אוֹתוֹ מֵאָה כֶסֶ״. And we spread an evil report and said, ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״. Each and every one of us must give a hundred silver. Reish Lakish said: you learn from the rapist, as it is written (ibid. 22:29), ״וְנָתַן הָאִישׁ הַשּׁוֹכֵב עִמָּהּ״ etc. And we raped the utterance ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״, and committed idolatry. Each one of us must give fifty silver. Rabbi Yehudah bar Simon said: you learn from a goring ox, as it says (Exodus 21:32), ״אִם עֶבֶד יִגַּח הַשּׁוֹר״ etc. And we exchanged His honor for an ox, as it says (Psalms 106:20), ״וַיָּמִירוּ אֶת כְּבוֹדָם בְּתַבְנִית שׁוֹר אוֹכֵל עֵשֶׂב״. Each one of us must give thirty shekel. The Holy One, blessed be He, knew what was in the heart of Moses; He said to him: By your life — not a talent of silver, not a hundred silver, not fifty shekels, and not thirty shekels, but rather ״זֶה יִתְּנוּ מַחֲצִית הַשֶּׁקֶל״ — end of quotation. And it appears that in this midrash there are many things that are not understood: did Moses then reckon with the event that took place in the days of Ahab king of Israel with the king of Aram? Also what is written, ״וַאֲנַחְנוּ אָנַסְנוּ הַדִּבּוּר״ — 'and we raped the utterance' — is lacking explanation, and the matter of the goring ox is difficult to understand." "And I say: since we find that in the matter of the calf Israel transgressed four categories of transgressions — and they are idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, bloodshed, and evil speech against them all, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וַיָּקוּמוּ לְצַחֵק״ (Exodus 32:6). And the evil speech is what is said: ״קוֹל עַנּוֹת אָנֹכִי שׁוֹמֵעַ״ (Exodus 32:18), that is, revilements and blasphemies, and this is what they said to one another: ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 32:4). For idolatry in general depends on the intention of the one who believes in it, for this reason it says (Ezekiel 14:5), ״לְמַעַן תְּפֹשׂ אֶת בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּלִבָּם״. And this was not enough for them, but they recounted the matter with their mouths like one who spreads an evil report about his Maker, and said ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — and not YHWH brought about all this — referring to the matter of the going up from Egypt. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, ״וְנָתְנוּ אִישׁ כֹּפֶר נַפְשׁוֹ״ (Exodus 30:12), Moses was in doubt concerning which sin of these four transgressions the atonement would be — whether for idolatry, or for bloodshed, or for forbidden sexual relations, or for evil speech." "The opinion of Rabbi Yehudah bar Ilai is that Moses thought, presumably, that this atonement would be for idolatry, and then it would certainly be a full talent of silver. And he understood this from what is said, ״כִּי תִשָּׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and along the lines of what is derived in the Yalkut in this portion, that the initial letters of all the tribes add up to five hundred thousand and ninety-seven thousand, and where are another three thousand to complete six hundred thousand? They are those who fell at the calf. And Moses thought: what does this come to teach? The Holy One, blessed be He, hinted to him to take the head-count of all the tribes of Israel and to know from within it that three thousand fell at the calf — is this anything but that HaShem wanted him to understand from this: just as those three thousand were an atonement for all the bodies of all Israel, so the atonement of the souls would also be in this number — that is, a talent of silver whose count comes to three thousand shekel, as Rashi explained in the portion Eileh Pekudei (Exodus 38:24). And if you say: from where do we know that this atonement would be specifically for idolatry and not for the other transgression among these four? Therefore he brings proof from the verse ״וְהָיְתָה נַפְשְׁךָ תַּחַת נַפְשׁוֹ אוֹ כִּכַּר כֶּסֶף תִּשְׁקוֹל״ (I Kings 20:39) — which was an atonement for sending a banished man from his grasp, and that was the king of Aram, who said ״אֱלֹהֵי הָרִים ה׳ וְלֹא אֱלֹהֵי עֲמָקִים״ (I Kings 20:28), for he denied HaShem and said that He does not attend to the lowly at all, but His Presence is in the heights of the heavens — and this was also the opinion of the makers of the calf. And since we find there a redemption of a soul for a talent of silver, if so certainly also here — what Moses thought, that perhaps HaShem wanted from each one a whole talent — was on account of the sin of idolatry." "Admittedly, the view of Rabbi Yosi is that Moses thought that this ransom was for evil speech and the echoing voices of the insults they uttered, bringing a bad name upon the Holy One, blessed be He, saying ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ (Exodus 32:4), in the manner explained above." "And the view of Resh Lakish is that Moses thought that this ransom was for forbidden sexual relations; therefore he said, 'From the case of the violated betrothed woman you learn,' and what is written 'and we compelled speech — you shall not have' — because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Mekhilta Yitro ch. 8) that the Decalogue tablets were five opposite five: 'You shall not have' opposite 'You shall not commit adultery,' etc., as will be explained below (Exodus 32:31) regarding the verse ״אָנָּא חָטָא הָעָם הַזֶּה חֲטָאָה גְדֹלָה״. And according to this, this depends on that: because they transgressed 'I am YHWH your Elohim,' they also transgressed 'You shall not murder'; and because they transgressed 'You shall not have,' they also transgressed 'You shall not commit adultery' — for this one was commanded opposite that one." "And the view of Rabbi Yehuda bar Simon is that Moses thought that perhaps this ransom was for bloodshed, for they had killed Hur with the law of a goring ox that kills a slave. For at a time when Israel do the will of the Place, they are called children of El the living (בְּנֵי אֵל חַי), and when they do not do His will, they are called slaves. Therefore Hur was assessed at the value of a slave, and Israel were compared to a goring ox, because it is written (Psalms 115:8) ״כְּמוֹהֶם יִהְיוּ עוֹשֵׂיהֶם״ etc. And if so, just as the essence of idolatry was an ox eating grass, so they descended from the level of man and came to the assessment of an ox. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said: not a talent etc. — because it is well known that in the removal of any evil thing, one must remove the cause, and automatically the result is nullified, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma Ḥukkat 8): for the atonement of the calf, let the mother come and wipe away her child's filth — for the red cow is the mother, who gave birth to the calf. When the root is uprooted, the branches fall away on their own; but if you cut the branches, there is reason to fear lest the root return, sprouting head and wormwood to grow new branches. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, said that the essence of this atonement is upon the root — that is, upon the thing that was the cause of all these transgressions. And this is: the abundance of gold and the lust for money brought them to this deed, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 86b) on ״וַיַּעֲשׂוּ לָהֶם אֱלֹהֵי זָהָב״ (Exodus 32:31) — Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: You caused it to them, because of the abundance of gold that You bestowed upon them, etc. And the explanation of the matter is thus: because He bestowed upon them an abundance of gold, therefore they became greedy and their eyes were never sated, and they sought for themselves the form of an ox — for the face of the ox is on the left in the divine chariot, and it is written (Job 37:22) ״מִצָּפוֹן זָהָב יֶאֱתֶה״. And they thought that the constellation of the ox enriches, and that it would bestow upon them still more abundance of gold, as it is written (Proverbs 14:4) ״וְרָב תְּבוּאוֹת בְּכֹחַ שׁוֹר״. And likewise Joseph the sustainer of all the land was called ״בְּכוֹר שׁוֹר״ (Deuteronomy 33:17). And therefore He commanded to give a half-shekel to atone for the one who covets money — for a person never has more than half in his hand, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ecclesiastes Rabbah 1:13): one who has a hundred mina desires two hundred; one who has two hundred desires four hundred — and thus it is found that a person never has in his hand more than a half-shekel." "And it could be that since this abundance came from the spoil of Egypt and the spoil of the sea, regarding which Solomon said ״תּוֹרֵי זָהָב נַעֲשֶׂה לָּךְ עִם נְקֻדּוֹת הַכָּסֶף״ (Song of Songs 1:11) — Rashi explained: נְקֻדּוֹת הַכֶּסֶף, that is the spoil of Egypt, and תּוֹרֵי זָהָב, that is the spoil of the sea. Therefore these two kinds of atonement came: let the red cow come and atone for the offspring — that is, this golden-red calf — and let these half-shekels of silver come to atone for the sin drawn from נְקֻדּוֹת הַכֶּסֶף. For at a time when an abundance of silver is found with its owners, then it adds craving upon craving, and one is forever poor in his own mind. And a hint to the matter: the letters of עָנִי ('poor') are adjacent to the letters of כֶּסֶף ('silver') in the alphabetical order. And this matter will be explained further in the verse ״כִּי פָרֻעַ הוּא״ (Exodus 32:25); see there."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {4 "The referent 'he' in 'he denied HaShem' is Ben-hadad king of Aram; 'a banished man' (איש חרמי) is the prophetic parable of I Kings 20 in which a prophet disguises himself and entrusts a captured prisoner to another man.", 7 "The verse citation 'ולא יהיה לך ואנחנו אנסנו את דיבור' combines language from the Decalogue with the concept of the violated betrothed woman (אונס); the reference appears to be building on the rabbinic pairing of commandments opposite each other on the two tablets. Also, the verse citation '(שמות לב לא)' attached to 'אלהי זהב' should refer to Exodus 32:31; rendered as written.", 8 "The letter adjacency observation: עָנִי (ayin-nun-yod) and כֶּסֶף (kaf-samekh-fei) — samekh follows nun and fei follows in sequence proximity to the letters of עני; rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:11.872554782Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}